      <section id="limitations" class="section-block limitations">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <div><p class="kicker">Honest limits</p><h2>What this can’t tell you</h2></div>
          <p>A good score means a model read public data well. It does not mean an AI understands the whole battlefield.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="limitations-grid">
          <article><h3>Public data only</h3><p>We poll the official Foxhole War API on a nominal fifteen-minute cadence, with coverage gaps possible. That keeps the playing field level and the whole experiment auditable — anyone can pull the same numbers.</p></article>
          <article><h3>No logistics intel</h3><p>Real Foxhole players would ask about shell stockpiles and supply lines. The public API doesn’t expose that, and we don’t scrape the game or run an in-game agent — so the models are forecasting with one hand tied behind their backs, on purpose.</p></article>
          <article><h3>Small on purpose</h3><p>Model calls cost money, so the experiment stays compact: a handful of models, eight calls per round. Treat everything here as an ongoing study, not a finished leaderboard.</p></article>
        </div>
      </section>
